{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROOT CAUSE — the filed premise (a lost CAS / non-fast-forward between the two clones) is\nfalsified by the job's own captured log.\n\nThe push was rejected by the RECEIVING repository, not by a ref race. The log carries\n`unresolved deltas left after unpacking`, `remote unpack failed: unpack-objects abnormal\nexit`, and `refs/heads/tickets does not point to a valid object!`. A sweep of the recent CI\nhistory found no `non-fast-forward`, no `cannot lock ref`, and no OOM/signal in that job.\nThe warning repeats 18 times with a monotonically climbing unpushed-commit counter, so the\nbare `remote.git` was already missing objects from the FIRST emit push: the remote was\ncorrupt before any invariant assertion ran.\n\nCross-check against the sibling occurrence recorded on chewed-illicit-blacklemur: that one\nfailed with clone B holding an EMPTY event set and a clean git log, with no push warnings at\nall. Different mechanism, already fixed by willing-cellular-carp. The corruption signature\nappears exactly once in the swept history, which is consistent with a rare race rather than\na deterministic defect.\n\nMECHANISM. Three facts compose:\n\n1. `two_clones` creates the bare remote with a plain `git init --bare` and never configures\n   it.\n2. `_make_repo` clones that local path without `--no-hardlinks`, so git's local optimization\n   HARDLINKS the remote's object files into each clone. Measured, not assumed: an assertion\n   over `(st_dev, st_ino)` pairs showed clone B sharing 19 object files with the remote.\n   `.tickets-tracker` is a linked worktree, so it shares its clone's object database.\n3. rebar's gc-config ensure unit keeps auto-gc enabled but forces it FOREGROUND\n   (`gc.autoDetach=false`, `maintenance.autoDetach=false`) exactly because a DETACHED\n   background gc/maintenance repacks an object database outside the write lock and corrupts\n   the store — bug 88eb, ADR 0051, recorded in the `rebar._store.sync` module docstring.\n\nThat pinning reaches every repository rebar owns. It does not reach a bare remote, which\nrebar does not own. So the harness's `remote.git` was the one repository in the fixture whose\n`receive-pack` ran `git gc --auto` at git's defaults — detached — after each of the ~50 emit\npushes, rewriting the object store the next push depended on. git-clone(1) separately warns\nthat the hardlink mode can race with concurrent modification of the source repository.\n\nTwo operations interleave, and both are on the REMOTE rather than between the clones: the\ndetached upkeep process spawned by push N, and the object transfer of push N+1.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT LOCALLY REPRODUCIBLE. Confirmed: the file passes standalone and under CI's\n`-n 4 --dist loadgroup`. Forcing `gc.auto=1` on the remote made gc run (108 loose objects\nrepacked) but did not corrupt it on this host, which is expected for a timing race that\nneeded one specific interleaving on one ubuntu-24.04 runner."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786565672718955001,"uuid":"37dad0a5-e127-4f5f-be11-ccaeca2f8dff"}